WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.440
- I got an email from the
world champion wingsuit team that said,

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:09.490
"Would you like to learn to fly
in the world's only diagonal wind tunnel?"

00:00:11.210 --> 00:00:14.210
I've got to start saying
no to these things.

00:00:16.648 --> 00:00:17.730
- My name is Jarno Cordia.

00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:19.400
As part of the Wingsuit Tunnel here

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:22.420
I've actually been setting up the coaching
here the last two years.

00:00:22.420 --> 00:00:26.600
And together now with Jenna, I teach here
and try to get everybody flying.

00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:29.575
Currently Jenna and myself,
we won the last World Cup in Italy

00:00:29.575 --> 00:00:32.420
in both indoor skydiving
and outdoor skydiving.

00:00:32.420 --> 00:00:35.510
We're at the moment the reigning team
in wingsuit acrobatics.

00:00:35.510 --> 00:00:37.920
This wind tunnel was around since 1935.

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:40.460
Used for testing aeroplanes ,
cars and those kind of things.

00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:44.100
I think they tested the Saab aeroplane and
the Huygens space capsule here.

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:46.210
This tunnel was up for auction
a few years ago

00:00:46.210 --> 00:00:50.060
and then four of the local Swedish guys,
who are also skydivers,

00:00:50.060 --> 00:00:52.750
they basically thought,
what if we can convert this

00:00:52.750 --> 00:00:55.953
into an angled wind tunnel
where you can actually fly wingsuits.

00:00:55.953 --> 00:00:58.200
And because that hadn't
been done yet, nobody

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:00.320
really knew if such a
thing would be possible.

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:03.240
So, they basically took the
horizontal wind tunnel here.

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:05.270
They put a small angled tube in it.

00:01:05.270 --> 00:01:07.290
Once they had done the initial testing,

00:01:07.290 --> 00:01:10.540
I was called in to actually see,
besides stable flying,

00:01:10.540 --> 00:01:12.170
what else can we do in the tunnel.

00:01:12.170 --> 00:01:14.690
When that was all a big success,
they raised the money

00:01:14.690 --> 00:01:16.460
and actually built this amazing machine.

00:01:16.460 --> 00:01:20.380
Now it's essentially an angled
tube that is on a pivot point

00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:23.213
and that can actually rotate
the tunnel up and down with a piston.

00:01:23.213 --> 00:01:27.960
I would say in terms of physical workout
for a beginner, it's insanely heavy.

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:30.347
And that is mainly
because, in the beginning,

00:01:30.347 --> 00:01:31.940
you are not flying super efficient.

00:01:31.940 --> 00:01:34.050
So you're using a lot of muscle tension.

00:01:34.050 --> 00:01:37.080
You're tending to bend the arm
which creates a round wing

00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:40.160
that really creates
drag on your body.

00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:43.230
In the beginning, maybe 40
minutes a day is the max

00:01:43.230 --> 00:01:45.610
and then people really feel like
they ran three marathons.

00:01:45.610 --> 00:01:46.960
I currently have the world record,

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:49.640
which is definitely the most
boring thing I ever did,

00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:52.230
but somehow the most
watched thing, and it's

00:01:52.230 --> 00:01:54.185
flying six and a half hours
non-stop in the tunnel

00:01:54.185 --> 00:01:57.069
without touching the
floor, walls or anything.

00:01:57.069 --> 00:01:59.413
- I can't think right now.

00:02:00.299 --> 00:02:01.429
Ah.

00:02:01.429 --> 00:02:03.403
The last 10 seconds, you
were giving me instructions

00:02:03.403 --> 00:02:06.391
but my brain just wasn't
parsing anything.

00:02:07.350 --> 00:02:09.330
- What we start with is this cable system

00:02:09.330 --> 00:02:11.716
where essentially you're
on two pulleys that are on

00:02:11.716 --> 00:02:14.220
the ceiling that allow you
to move around, but they

00:02:14.220 --> 00:02:16.910
don't allow you to actually
get close to the walls.

00:02:16.910 --> 00:02:19.300
The cables definitely influence
you a little bit as well

00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:21.810
with like the bounciness
of cables, but it

00:02:21.810 --> 00:02:26.730
essentially limits the motion
to safely learn to just control your body.

00:02:26.730 --> 00:02:27.770
Kind of like an aeroplane.

00:02:27.770 --> 00:02:30.810
Like you lean a bit left,
the wing goes left.

00:02:30.810 --> 00:02:33.440
You raise the chin and the chest gets air.

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:35.800
That changes your angle,
pushes you backward.

00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:37.270
With those kind of motions, you need to

00:02:37.270 --> 00:02:39.160
really learn to fly.

00:02:39.160 --> 00:02:41.934
And even for skydivers that have a lot
of wingsuit experience,

00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:44.080
they tend to fly more by instinct

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:46.310
and even for them, this
is a lot more precise

00:02:46.310 --> 00:02:48.030
than what you normally do outdoors.

00:02:48.030 --> 00:02:50.384
- So am I too far back or too far forward?

00:02:50.384 --> 00:02:51.240
- Too far forward.

00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:54.900
From that point where you can actually
control yourself on the cables,

00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:58.890
there you can continue to
flying without the cables.

00:02:58.890 --> 00:03:00.750
There's a small, intermediate step we do,

00:03:00.750 --> 00:03:03.040
which is flying with a leash on the belly

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:05.130
Which allows us to let people fly free but

00:03:05.130 --> 00:03:07.320
in case they do anything less smart,

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:09.300
we can still put them on the floor.

00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:10.397
The difference between
flying in the tunnel

00:03:10.397 --> 00:03:13.300
and flying in the air is
definitely the lack of space.

00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:15.620
So in the air, if you're
learning to fly and you're

00:03:15.620 --> 00:03:18.360
essentially all over the
place, that is not an issue.

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:20.100
In the tunnel if you're
all over the place,

00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:21.950
you can be all over the
place for about two metres

00:03:21.950 --> 00:03:23.220
and then the wall stops you.

00:03:23.220 --> 00:03:25.050
Another thing that is
definitely different is

00:03:25.050 --> 00:03:26.880
your reflexes that you
have from the ground.

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:29.080
Like if you're flying on your back
and you're falling backwards,

00:03:29.080 --> 00:03:31.180
we tend to put our butt down to cushion our fall,

00:03:31.197 --> 00:03:33.540
which of course in the sky,
you would never do.

00:03:33.540 --> 00:03:37.550
And definitely the walls and the floor,
they introduce these reflexes

00:03:37.550 --> 00:03:39.730
that we've trained over the years

00:03:39.730 --> 00:03:42.185
to put your arms in front
of you or to the sides

00:03:42.185 --> 00:03:44.830
that in flying don't have the best effect.

00:03:44.830 --> 00:03:48.210
A big thing that the tunnel brings
that skydiving doesn't get you

00:03:48.210 --> 00:03:51.840
is the amount of training you can do
and the reference you get

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:55.690
where normally in the sky,
if I'm flying with a training partner,

00:03:55.690 --> 00:03:57.860
when we're having a little bit of movement
between each other,

00:03:57.860 --> 00:04:00.060
we don't see who is making the movement.

00:04:00.060 --> 00:04:02.363
In the tunnel, because you
have a static reference,

00:04:02.363 --> 00:04:05.560
you see every little move so you learn

00:04:05.560 --> 00:04:08.200
so much faster because
this gives you so much more feedback

00:04:08.200 --> 00:04:09.740
in terms of what you're doing.

00:04:09.740 --> 00:04:12.720
Once they showed that they can fly well
with the belly leash,

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:15.280
that comes off as well and then
you're flying fully free.

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:17.760
And then from there it's
quite a rapid progression

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:19.200
to learn to fly on your back

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:21.240
and then actually learn
to do the transitions,

00:04:21.240 --> 00:04:22.930
carving around the tunnel.

00:04:22.930 --> 00:04:26.200
Typically that whole
progression takes around

00:04:26.200 --> 00:04:29.110
two to three hours of
flying for most people.

00:04:29.110 --> 00:04:32.160
in general, it's quite cool to
see that where normally

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:34.940
people would have 200-300 skydives
before they could even try this,

00:04:34.940 --> 00:04:39.383
now we're taking people off the street
and teaching them to fly in one or two days.

00:04:42.819 --> 00:04:46.810
- I did not get to the point where, like,
I could just fly free in the tunnel,

00:04:46.810 --> 00:04:50.570
but there was four or five seconds
in there where I felt stable.

00:04:50.570 --> 00:04:52.620
And I don't think you were pulling on me?

00:04:52.620 --> 00:04:53.460
- No, no no.

00:04:54.760 --> 00:04:55.780
- Thank you.

00:04:55.780 --> 00:04:57.500
-You're more than welcome!
Super good, man.

00:04:57.503 --> 00:05:00.300
- Thank you very much.
I'm done!

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:04.840
- The amount of flying you did now is what
the super trained people usually do

00:05:04.840 --> 00:05:07.057
after building that stamina.

00:05:07.880 --> 00:05:12.100
So you flew more than most people do
on the first day in a very short time span

00:05:12.100 --> 00:05:15.200
so all the muscles you're feeling that
you've never used before, that's quite normal.

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:16.100
- Yeah!

00:05:16.100 --> 00:05:17.860
But you did really good.

00:05:17.870 --> 00:05:19.324
And you're going to sleep
really well tonight.

00:05:19.324 --> 00:05:20.780
- Yeah! Yeah I am!

00:05:21.860 --> 00:05:24.230
- Should we just turn off the lights here
and leave you here?

00:05:24.230 --> 00:05:27.400
- No, because then it's a slow slide
down into that!

00:05:27.400 --> 00:05:30.595
All right we're good. We got an outro.`
Thank you so much.

00:05:32.070 --> 00:05:33.843
All right, yeah.
We're good.

